  "radCheckTracheaCentral": "Is the trachea central?",
  "radCheckTracheaCentralDesc": "Check for deviation away from (tension pneumothorax) or towards (atelectasis) a lesion.",
  "radCheckLungsExpanded": "Are the lungs well expanded?",
  "radCheckLungsExpandedDesc": "Ensure you can see at least 10 posterior ribs to confirm good inspiration.",
  "radCheckCostophrenicAngles": "Are costophrenic angles sharp?",
  "radCheckCostophrenicAnglesDesc": "Blunting suggests pleural effusion or previous scarring.",
  "radCheckHeartSize": "Is the heart size normal?",
  "radCheckHeartSizeDesc": "Cardiothoracic Ratio (CTR) should be less than 0.5 on a PA film.",
  "radCheckDiaphragmPosition": "Is the diaphragm position normal?",
  "radCheckDiaphragmPositionDesc": "Right hemidiaphragm is usually slightly higher (due to the liver).",
  "radCheckBonesSoftTissues": "Bones and soft tissues unremarkable?",
  "radCheckBonesSoftTissuesDesc": "Check for fractures, surgical emphysema, or foreign bodies.",
  "radLearnMore": "Learn More",
  "radTipAirwayTitle": "Analyzing the Airway",
  "radTipAirwayDesc": "The trachea should be midline. Deviation *away* from a pathology suggests tension pneumothorax or large effusion. Deviation *towards* suggests collapse or fibrosis. Identify the carina (NG tubes should be below it).",
  "radTipBreathingTitle": "Evaluating the Lungs",
  "radTipBreathingDesc": "Count the ribs: 10 posterior ribs indicate good inspiration. Compare lung fields for symmetry in density. Look for pleural lines (pneumothorax) or consolidations (pneumonia).",
  "radTipCirculationTitle": "Heart & Mediastinum",
  "radTipCirculationDesc": "The Cardiothoracic Ratio (CTR) should be < 50% on a PA film. The 'Silhouette Sign' helps localize opacities: if the right heart border is lost, the opacity is in the middle lobe.",
  "radTipDiaphragmTitle": "The Diaphragm",
  "radTipDiaphragmDesc": "The right hemidiaphragm is higher than the left. 'Air under the diaphragm' (pneumoperitoneum) is a surgical emergency. Ensure costophrenic angles are acute and not blunted.",
  "radTipEverythingElseTitle": "Extra-thoracic Structures",
  "radTipEverythingElseDesc": "Scan the bones (ribs, clavicles) for fractures or lytic lesions. Check soft tissues for surgical emphysema (air pockets). Finally, systematically trace every line, tube, and drain.",
  "radChapterDiagnosisGuide": "Diagnosis Guide",
  "radDiagnosisPneumonia": "Pneumonia",
  "radDiagnosisPneumoniaDesc": "Look for consolidation, air bronchograms, and silhouette signs.",
  "radDiagnosisHeartFailure": "Heart Failure (Edema)",
  "radDiagnosisHeartFailureDesc": "Look for cardiomegaly, Kerley B lines, and pleural effusion.",
  "radDiagnosisPneumothorax": "Pneumothorax",
  "radDiagnosisPneumothoraxDesc": "Identify a visible pleural line without distal lung markings.",
  "radDiagnosisEffusion": "Pleural Effusion",
  "radDiagnosisEffusionDesc": "Look for blunted costophrenic angles and meniscus signs.",
  "radDiagnosisAtelectasis": "Atelectasis (Collapse)",
  "radDiagnosisAtelectasisDesc": "Look for volume loss, tracheal deviation towards the lesion, and linear opacities.",
  "radSyndromeParietal": "Parietal Syndrome",
  "radSyndromeParietalDesc": "**Definition**:\nThe set of radiological signs corresponding to a lesion of the thoracic wall (soft tissues or bones).\n\n**Radiological Semiology**:\n* **Soft tissues**: Peripheral opacity of watery density, homogeneous, sharp contour if tangential, smooth 'pente douce' connection.\n* **Bone lesions**: Osteolytic or condensing (bone lysis, notch-type rib erosion, pathological fracture).\n\n**Description of images (Illustrated Semiology)**:\n* **Peripheral opacity**: Water-density, homogeneous.\n* **Wall connection**: Smooth 'pente douce' with an obtuse connection angle.\n* **Pleural line sign**: Sharp interface with the lung parenchyma.\n* **Dimensions**: Horizontal diameter (base) of the opacity almost as large as its vertical diameter.\n* **Associated signs**: Bone lesions (rib lysis) often present; the opacity moves with the rib cage.",
  "radSyndromePleural": "Pleural Syndrome",
  "radSyndromePleuralDesc": "**Definition**:\nSigns reflecting the presence of liquid, air, or abnormal tissue between the pleural layers.\n\n**Semiology**:\n* **Free liquid effusion (Pleurisy)**: Basal opacity in a sheet, effaces costophrenic angles. Damoiseau's curve (concave upwards). Mobile with position changes. No bronchogram.\n* **Pneumothorax (Air)**: Peripheral avascular hyperlucency (black area without markings), visceral pleural line (fine bordering line).\n* **Hydro-pneumothorax**: Horizontal air-fluid level.\n\n**Description of images (Illustrated Semiology)**:\n* **Damoiseau's line**: Upper limit concave upwards and inwards of the liquid effusion.\n* **Avascular hyperlucency**: Black area without any vascular markings (PTX).\n* **Bordering line**: Fine line corresponding to the visceral pleura limiting the lung retracted towards the hilum.",
  "radSyndromeAlveolar": "Alveolar Syndrome",
  "radSyndromeAlveolarDesc": "**Definition**:\nFilling of the alveoli with fluid (water, pus, blood) or cells.\n\n**Characteristic Semiology**:\n* **Water-density opacity**.\n* **Blurred limits** (unless against a fissure).\n* **Confluence** (diffusion via Kohn pores).\n* **Systematization** (specific anatomical territory).\n* **Air bronchogram**: Patent bronchi within the opacity.\n\n**Description of images (Illustrated Semiology)**:\n* **Cloudy or cottony appearance**: Typical blurred-limit opacities.\n* **Confluence**: Tendency of lesions to merge.\n* **Bronchogram & Alveologram**: Visibility of clear bronchi and alveoli within the white opacity.\n* **Butterfly wings**: Symmetrical perihilar opacities respecting the periphery (Pulmonary Edema).",
  "radSyndromeInterstitial": "Interstitial Syndrome",
  "radSyndromeInterstitialDesc": "**Definition**:\nPresence of fluid or abnormal cells in the pulmonary interstitium.\n\n**Semiological Features**:\n* **Sharp limits**.\n* **Non-confluent**, non-systematized.\n* **No air bronchogram**.\n* Slow evolution.\n\n**Description of images (Illustrated Semiology)**:\n* **Kerley lines (B)**: Septal thickening visible at the base as short horizontal lines.\n* **Reticulations**: 'Net-like' mesh appearance.\n* **Ground glass**: Subtle increase in lung density not effacing the vessels.\n* **Honeycombing**: Grouped small cystic lucencies (terminal stage of fibrosis).",
  "radSyndromeBronchial": "Bronchial Syndrome",
  "radSyndromeBronchialDesc": "**Definition**:\nSet of signs related to bronchial pathology (thickening, dilation, or obstruction).\n\n**Direct Signs**:\n* **Tram lines and Rings**: Thickening of the bronchial walls.\n* **Signet ring**: Bronchial diameter > Adjacent artery diameter.\n\n**Indirect Signs**:\n* **Atelectasis**: Systematic retractile opacity.\n* **Trapping**: Localized expiratory hyperlucency.\n\n**Description of images (Illustrated Semiology)**:\n* **Tram line sign**: Wall thickening in longitudinal view.\n* **Ring sign**: Wall thickening in transverse view.\n* **Signet ring sign**: Bronchial dilation (bronchiectasis) with bronchial diameter greater than the artery.\n* **Retractile opacity**: Atelectasis with mediastinal shift and fissural displacement towards the lesion.",
  "radSyndromeCavitary": "Cavitary Syndrome",
  "radSyndromeCavitaryDesc": "**Definition**:\nPresence of a lucency (cavity) within an area of opacity or the parenchyma.\n\n**Semiology**:\n* **Wall**: Fixed or variable thickness. Thin (< 3mm) or thick (> 15mm).\n* **Air-fluid level**: Horizontal line (air above, liquid below).\n* **Grelot sign**: Mobile mass inside the cavity.\n\n**Description of images (Illustrated Semiology)**:\n* **Lucency image**: Area of transparency (black) within the parenchyma.\n* **Air-fluid level**: Strict horizontal limit between air and liquid.\n* **Grelot sign**: Mobile mass (e.g., aspergillome) within a cavity.\n* **Nénuphar sign**: Floating membrane typical of a ruptured hydatid cyst.",
  "radSyndromeMediastinal": "Mediastinal Syndrome",
  "radSyndromeMediastinalDesc": "**Definition**:\nSigns reflecting the presence of a mass or effusion in the mediastinum.\n\n**Localization Signs**:\n* **Sharp, convex outer border** towards the lung.\n* **Silhouette sign**: Effacement of a border in contact with a structure of the same density.\n* **Iceberg sign**: Thoraco-abdominal mass departing from the spine.\n\n**Description of images (Illustrated Semiology)**:\n* **Silhouette sign**: Blurring of borders between two structures of same density in contact.\n* **Cervicothoracic sign**: Opacity exceeding the upper border of the clavicle (indicates posterior position).\n* **Iceberg sign (thoraco-abdominal)**: Mass departing from the spine below the diaphragm.\n* **Hilar overlay sign**: Visibility of vessels through the opacity (indicates ant/post position relative to the hilum).",
